 2. TERMINAL (GNOME) SHORTCUTS
================================================================================

super + t                  launch terminal (custom shortcut; default on Ubuntu is ctrl + alt + t)
shift + ctrl + t           open a new tab
shift + ctrl + n           open a new window
shift + ctrl + w           close current tab
ctrl + shift + c / v       copy / paste inside the terminal (ctrl+c is SIGINT!)

zoom in:                   ctrl + +   (ctrl + shift + =)
zoom out:                  ctrl + -
reset zoom:                ctrl + 0

left tab:                  ctrl + shift + page up      (move current tab to the left)
right tab:                 ctrl + shift + page down    (move current tab to the right)
previous tab:              ctrl + page up
next tab:                  ctrl + page down
jump to tab N:             alt + 1..9

ctrl + l                   same as the `clear` command (clears the screen, keeps the line you typed)


================================================================================
 3. BASH SHORTCUTS & HISTORY
================================================================================

ctrl + r
    Reverse-i-search: type part of a previous command to search history.
    Press ctrl + r again to go further back, Enter to run, ctrl + g / Esc to cancel.

ctrl + c        interrupt (kill) the running foreground command (SIGINT)
ctrl + z        suspend the foreground command (resume it with `fg` or `bg`)
ctrl + d        end of input / logout of the shell (when the line is empty)
ctrl + a        move cursor to the start of the line
ctrl + e        move cursor to the end of the line
alt + b / f     move one word back / forward
ctrl + w        delete the word before the cursor
ctrl + u        delete from cursor to start of line
ctrl + k        delete from cursor to end of line
ctrl + y        paste ("yank") what was last deleted with ctrl+w/u/k
tab tab         autocomplete / show all completions

history
    Prints the numbered command history.
    $ history | grep docker                # find docker commands you ran before

!!              repeat the last command          $ sudo !!    # rerun last command with sudo
!$              last argument of previous cmd    $ mkdir project && cd !$
!n              run command number n from history  $ !512
cd -            go back to the previous directory


================================================================================
 4. PYTHON VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS & `source`
================================================================================

python3 -m venv .venv
    Creates a virtual environment in the folder .venv (isolated python + pip).
    $ python3 -m venv .venv

source .venv/bin/activate
    Activates the virtual environment: `python` and `pip` now point to .venv.
    Your prompt usually shows (.venv).
    $ source .venv/bin/activate && pip install -r requirements.txt

deactivate
    Leaves the active virtual environment (back to the system python).
    $ deactivate

source  (same as: . file)
    - Runs a script in the CURRENT shell environment instead of starting a new
      one (a new shell = `bash script.sh` / `./script.sh`).
    - Executes commands from a file in the current shell, so variables,
      functions, aliases and `cd` from that file stay in effect afterwards.
    $ source ~/.bashrc                     # reload bashrc after editing it
    $ source .env                          # load variables from a .env file


================================================================================
 5. GETTING HELP / LOCATING COMMANDS
================================================================================

help
    Help for bash BUILT-IN commands (cd, export, source, for, if...), which have
    no own man page.
    $ help cd

man [command]
    Full command manual. Inside: / to search, n next match, q quit.
    $ man find
    $ man -k partition                     # search all man pages for a keyword

[command] --help
    Most programs print a short usage summary.
    $ grep --help | less

which
    Locates a command (for PROGRAMS / EXECUTABLES). Searches the folders in
    $PATH in order and prints the first match - i.e. the one that will run.
    $ which python3                        # -> /usr/bin/python3
    $ which -a python                      # show ALL matches in $PATH

type
    Like which, but also knows about aliases, functions and builtins.
    $ type ll                              # ll is aliased to `ls -alF'

echo $PATH
    Shows the list of folders searched for executables (separated by :).
    $ export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH" # prepend a folder to PATH


================================================================================
 6. FILES & DIRECTORIES
================================================================================

ls
    Lists directory contents.
    $ ls -lah                              # long format, all (hidden) files, human sizes
    $ ls -lt                               # sort by modification time, newest first

cd / pwd
    Change directory / print working directory.
    $ cd ~/code/Notes ; pwd

mkdir
    Create directories.
    $ mkdir -p src/utils/tests             # -p creates parent dirs, no error if it exists

touch
    Creates an empty file, or updates the timestamp of an existing file.
    $ touch notes.txt

cp
    Copy files / directories.
    $ cp file.txt backup.txt
    $ cp -r project/ project_backup/       # -r recursive (needed for folders)

mv
    Move or rename files.
    $ mv old_name.txt new_name.txt
    $ mv *.jpg ~/Pictures/

rm
    Delete files. There is no trash - be careful.
    $ rm file.txt
    $ rm -i *.log                          # ask before every delete

rm -r
    Deletes a folder and everything inside it (recursive).
    $ rm -r build/
    $ rm -rf node_modules/                 # -f: force, no prompts, ignore missing

rmdir
    Deletes an EMPTY directory only (safe).
    $ rmdir empty_folder

symlinks (ln -s)
    A symbolic link is a pointer/shortcut to another file or folder. Deleting
    the link does not delete the target; if the target is moved the link breaks.
    `ln` without -s makes a hard link (another name for the same data).
    $ ln -s /path/to/target linkname       # create symlink "linkname" -> target
    $ ln -sf ~/dotfiles/.bashrc ~/.bashrc  # -f overwrite an existing link
    $ ls -l linkname                       # shows: linkname -> /path/to/target
    $ readlink -f linkname                 # print the final resolved path
    $ find . -xtype l                      # find broken symlinks

tree
    Shows a directory as a tree (sudo apt install tree).
    $ tree -L 2 -I node_modules            # 2 levels deep, ignore node_modules

file
    Tells you what kind of file something is (by content, not extension).
    $ file mystery.bin

stat
    Detailed file info: size, permissions, owner, access/modify times.
    $ stat file.txt


================================================================================
 7. PERMISSIONS & OWNERSHIP
================================================================================

    Permission string:  -rwxr-xr--   = type | owner | group | others
    Numbers:            r=4, w=2, x=1  -> add them per group (7=rwx, 6=rw-, 5=r-x, 4=r--)

chmod 644 [file]
    Sets permissions numerically: owner rw (6), group r (4), others r (4).
    Typical for normal files. 755 is typical for scripts/folders, 600 for
    private keys.
    $ chmod 644 index.html
    $ chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    $ chmod -R 755 public/                 # -R recursive

chmod +x
    Makes a file executable (for everyone). u+x = only owner, g-w = remove
    group write, etc.
    $ chmod +x deploy.sh && ./deploy.sh

chown
    Changes the owner (and optionally group) of files.
    $ sudo chown matej file.txt
    $ sudo chown -R matej:matej /var/www/app    # owner:group, recursive

sudo
    Run a command as root (administrator).
    $ sudo apt update
    $ sudo -i                              # open a root shell (exit with `exit`)


================================================================================
 8. VIEWING & PROCESSING TEXT
================================================================================

cat
    Prints the contents of a file (conCATenates files to stdout).
    $ cat config.yaml
    $ cat part1.txt part2.txt > whole.txt  # join files
    $ cat -n script.sh                     # with line numbers

less
    Scrollable file viewer (doesn't load the whole file). / search, q quit,
    G end, g start, F follow like tail -f.
    $ less /var/log/syslog

head
    Prints the start of a file (first 10 lines by default).
    $ head -n 20 data.csv

tail
    Prints the end of a file (last 10 lines by default).
    $ tail -n 50 app.log
    $ tail -f app.log                      # follow: keep printing new lines (logs!)

sort
    Sorts lines alphabetically (or numerically with -n).
    $ sort names.txt
    $ sort -n -r numbers.txt               # numeric, reversed
    $ sort -t, -k2 data.csv                # sort CSV by 2nd column

uniq
    Removes ADJACENT duplicate lines - so almost always use after `sort`.
    $ sort names.txt | uniq
    $ sort access.log | uniq -c | sort -rn | head    # count occurrences, top 10

wc
    Counts lines, words, bytes.
    $ wc -l *.py                           # number of lines per file

cut
    Extracts columns/fields from each line.
    $ cut -d, -f1,3 data.csv               # fields 1 and 3 from a CSV
    $ cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd              # list of user names

tr
    Translates or deletes characters.
    $ echo "hello" | tr a-z A-Z            # -> HELLO
    $ tr -d '\r' < win.txt > unix.txt      # remove Windows line endings

sed
    Stream editor - performs basic text transformations on an input stream,
    line by line (sed = line editor). Most used for search & replace.
    $ sed 's/foo/bar/g' file.txt           # print with every foo replaced by bar
    $ sed -i 's/localhost/127.0.0.1/g' config.ini   # -i edit file in place
    $ sed -n '10,20p' file.txt             # print only lines 10-20
    $ sed '/^#/d' file.txt                 # delete comment lines
    # rename files (change names of files):
    $ for f in *.jpeg; do mv "$f" "$(echo "$f" | sed 's/\.jpeg$/.jpg/')"; done

awk
    Like sed but for parsing: splits each line into fields ($1, $2, ... $NF)
    and is a small programming language for column-based data.
    $ awk '{print $1}' access.log          # first column (space separated)
    $ awk -F, '{print $2}' data.csv        # -F sets separator
    $ awk '$3 > 100 {print $1, $3}' data   # filter rows by condition
    $ awk '{sum += $5} END {print sum}' f  # sum a column

xargs
    Turns input lines into arguments for another command.
    $ find . -name "*.tmp" | xargs rm
    $ cat urls.txt | xargs -n1 -P4 wget    # 4 parallel downloads

tee
    Writes input to a file AND to the screen.
    $ make 2>&1 | tee build.log
    $ echo "line" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts # append to a root-owned file

diff
    Shows differences between two files.
    $ diff -u old.txt new.txt
    $ diff -r dir1/ dir2/                  # compare directories

Redirection & pipes
    >   write stdout to file (overwrite)        $ ls > files.txt
    >>  append stdout to file                   $ echo "x" >> log.txt
    2>  redirect stderr                         $ cmd 2> errors.txt
    &>  redirect stdout + stderr                $ cmd &> all.txt
    |   pipe stdout into next command           $ ps aux | grep python
    /dev/null  discard output                   $ cmd > /dev/null 2>&1


================================================================================
 9. SEARCHING (grep / find)
================================================================================

grep
    Searches for lines matching a pattern in files. Takes in a regular expression.
    $ grep "error" app.log
    $ grep -n "TODO" *.py                  # -n show line numbers
    $ grep -v "DEBUG" app.log              # -v invert: lines NOT matching
    $ grep -E "error|warn" app.log         # -E extended regex
    $ grep -c "404" access.log             # count matching lines
    $ grep -A3 -B2 "Exception" app.log     # 3 lines After, 2 lines Before match

grep -i -r
    -i case insensitive, -r recursive through all files in a directory.
    $ grep -ir "password" ./config
    $ grep -rl "import requests" .         # -l only print file names
    $ grep -rn --include="*.js" "fetch(" src/

find
    Finds files / directories by name, type, size, time... and can run
    commands on them.
    $ find . -type f -name "filename"      # files with exact name, from current dir
    $ find . -type f -iname "*.jpg"        # case-insensitive wildcard
    $ find . -type d -name node_modules    # directories
    $ find . -size +100M                   # bigger than 100 MB
    $ find . -mtime -1                     # modified in the last 24h
    $ find . -name "*.pyc" -delete         # delete matches
    $ find . -name "*.sh" -exec chmod +x {} \;   # run command on each match

locate
    Very fast file search using a prebuilt database (sudo apt install plocate;
    refresh db with `sudo updatedb`).
    $ locate nginx.conf


================================================================================
 10. HARDWARE & SYSTEM INFO
================================================================================

lspci
    Lists all PCI devices (hardware connected via the PCI bus like GPU, network
    card, etc.). PCI = Peripheral Component Interconnect.
    $ lspci | grep -i vga                  # which GPU do I have
    $ lspci -k                             # also show kernel driver in use

lsusb
    Lists USB devices.
    $ lsusb

lsblk
    Lists block devices (disks, partitions, USB sticks) and where they're mounted.
    $ lsblk -f                             # also filesystem type & UUID

rfkill list
    Checks whether wireless devices (like Wi-Fi or Bluetooth) are blocked
    (soft = by software/switch in settings, hard = physical switch) or enabled.
    $ rfkill list
    $ sudo rfkill unblock wifi             # unblock wi-fi
    $ sudo rfkill unblock all

sudo dmesg
    Prints or controls the kernel ring buffer - kernel messages about hardware,
    drivers, USB plug-ins, errors.
    $ sudo dmesg -T | tail -30             # -T human-readable timestamps
    $ sudo dmesg -w                        # follow live (then plug in a device)
    $ sudo dmesg | grep -i usb

uname
    Kernel / OS info.
    $ uname -a

lsb_release -a  /  cat /etc/os-release
    Which distro and version you're running.

lscpu / nproc
    CPU info / number of CPU cores.
    $ nproc

uptime
    How long the system is running + load average.
    $ uptime


================================================================================
 11. PROCESSES & SYSTEM MONITORING
================================================================================

$(command)
    Command substitution: runs command and captures its output so it can be
    used as a value.
    $ echo "Today is $(date +%F)"
    $ kill $(lsof -t -i:3000)              # kill whatever runs on port 3000

&
    Runs a command in the background, so the terminal is free immediately.
    $ python3 -m http.server 8000 &
    $ jobs                                 # list background jobs of this shell
    $ fg %1                                # bring job 1 to the foreground
    $ bg                                   # resume a ctrl+z-suspended job in background

nohup
    Keep a command running after you close the terminal / ssh session.
    $ nohup ./long_task.sh > task.log 2>&1 &

ps
    Process status - snapshot of running processes.
    $ ps                                   # processes of the current shell
    $ ps aux                               # ALL processes of all users, with CPU/MEM
    $ ps aux | grep java

ps -a
    For all processes attached to a terminal (not only this shell).
    $ ps -a

ps -p [pid] -f
    Check a specific process by its PID; -f = full format (shows the full
    command line, parent PID, start time).
    $ ps -p 22977 -f

pgrep / pkill
    Find / kill processes by name.
    $ pgrep -a node                        # list PIDs + command lines matching "node"
    $ pkill -f "python app.py"

kill
    Sends a signal to a process (default SIGTERM = ask nicely to stop).
    $ kill 22977
    $ kill -9 22977                        # SIGKILL - force kill, can't be ignored
    $ killall firefox

top
    Displays Linux tasks live (CPU, memory, processes). Inside: q quit,
    M sort by memory, P sort by CPU, k kill.
    $ top
    $ top -p 22977                         # monitor only one PID

htop
    Nicer, colorful, interactive top (sudo apt install htop).
    $ htop

watch
    Re-runs a command every N seconds and shows the output full-screen.
    $ watch -n 2 'df -h'
    $ watch -n 1 nvidia-smi

lsof
    (list open files) Linux/Unix utility to identify which processes
    are using specific files, directories, or network ports. Because
    everything is a file in Unix-like systems, lsof can also track network
    sockets, devices, and pipes.
    $ lsof /var/log/syslog                 # who has this file open
    $ lsof -u matej                        # all files opened by a user

lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN
    See which ports servers are listening on. -i network files, -P show port
    numbers (not names), -n don't resolve hostnames (faster).
    $ sudo lsof -i -P -n | grep LISTEN

lsof -t -i:3000
    Returns only the PID (-t terse) of the service on port 3000.
    $ lsof -t -i:3000
    $ kill $(lsof -t -i:3000)              # free up port 3000


