 12. DISK & MEMORY
================================================================================

df
    Disk space (free/used) per mounted filesystem.
    $ df -h                                # -h human readable (G, M)

du
    Directory usage - how much space files/folders take.
    $ du -sh ~/Downloads                   # -s summary total only
    $ du -h --max-depth=1 . | sort -h      # size of each subfolder, sorted

ncdu
    Interactive disk-usage browser - best way to find what fills your disk
    (sudo apt install ncdu).
    $ ncdu ~

free
    Free and used memory (RAM and swap).
    $ free -h

mount / umount
    Attach / detach a filesystem.
    $ sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb
    $ sudo umount /mnt/usb


================================================================================
 13. SERVICES & LOGS (systemd)
================================================================================

systemctl
    Controls systemd services (daemons).
    $ systemctl status nginx
    $ sudo systemctl start|stop|restart nginx
    $ sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql   # start now and on every boot
    $ systemctl list-units --type=service --state=running

journalctl
    Reads system/service logs collected by systemd.
    $ journalctl -u nginx -f               # follow logs of one service
    $ journalctl -b -p err                 # errors since last boot
    $ journalctl --since "1 hour ago"


================================================================================
 14. NETWORKING
================================================================================

ip a
    Shows the protocol (IPv4 or IPv6) addresses on every network device
    (short for `ip address`).
    $ ip a
    $ ip -br a                             # brief, one line per interface
    $ ip r                                 # routing table / default gateway

ifconfig
    Old (deprecated) tool for showing/configuring network interfaces - same info
    as `ip a`. Part of net-tools (sudo apt install net-tools).
    $ ifconfig
    $ ifconfig wlp2s0

ping
    Sends ICMP echo requests to a host - checks if it's reachable and how fast.
    $ ping google.com
    $ ping -c 4 8.8.8.8                    # only 4 packets

curl
    Transfers data from or to a server using various protocols like HTTP,
    HTTPS, FTP, and more. The go-to tool for testing APIs.
    $ curl https://example.com
    $ curl -I https://example.com          # only response headers
    $ curl -v http://localhost:8080/health # verbose: see request/response
    $ curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
           -d '{"name":"matej"}' http://localhost:3000/api/users
    $ curl -L -o file.zip https://.../file.zip   # follow redirects, save to file
    $ curl -k https://localhost:8443       # ignore self-signed cert

wget
    Downloads files from the web (good for big/recursive downloads, resumes).
    $ wget https://example.com/file.iso
    $ wget -c https://example.com/file.iso # continue an interrupted download

ssh
    Secure shell - log in to / run commands on a remote machine.
    $ ssh user@192.168.1.10
    $ ssh -p 2222 user@host                # custom port
    $ ssh -i ~/.ssh/mykey user@host        # specific key
    $ ssh user@host 'df -h'                # run one command remotely
    $ ssh -L 5432:localhost:5432 user@host # tunnel: remote port 5432 -> local 5432
    $ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "jetam.puc@gmail.com"   # create a key pair
    $ ssh-copy-id user@host                # install your key -> no more passwords

scp
    Transfers (copies) files between systems over ssh.
    $ scp file.txt user@host:/home/user/
    $ scp user@host:/var/log/app.log .     # download
    $ scp -r folder/ user@host:~/          # whole folder

rsync
    Smarter copy (local or over ssh): only transfers what changed, can resume.
    $ rsync -avz --progress src/ user@host:~/backup/src/
    $ rsync -av --delete ~/Documents/ /mnt/usb/Documents/  # mirror

ss
    Modern netstat - shows sockets / listening ports.
    $ ss -tulpn                            # tcp+udp, listening, with process, numeric

dig / nslookup
    DNS lookups.
    $ dig example.com +short
    $ nslookup example.com

nc (netcat)
    Read/write raw TCP/UDP connections - test if a port is open.
    $ nc -zv localhost 5432                # is port 5432 open?

python3 -m http.server
    Instantly serves the current folder over HTTP (sharing files on LAN).
    $ python3 -m http.server 8000


================================================================================
 15. PACKET CAPTURE (tcpdump / Wireshark)
================================================================================

    TCPDUMP
    Command-line packet sniffer: prints packets passing through an interface.

    sudo tcpdump -i lo -A 'tcp port 8443'
        -i -> interface, lo -> loopback address: 127.0.0.1,
        -A displays packet contents as ASCII.
        Example: watch the traffic of a local server on port 8443.

    $ sudo tcpdump -D                      # list available interfaces
    $ sudo tcpdump -i any port 80          # all interfaces, HTTP
    $ sudo tcpdump -i wlp2s0 host 192.168.1.5   # traffic to/from one host
    $ sudo tcpdump -n -c 100 -i eth0       # -n no DNS resolve, -c stop after 100
    $ sudo tcpdump -i any -w capture.pcap  # save to file -> open in Wireshark


