 18. MEDIA / PHOTOS
================================================================================

exiftool -b -JpgFromRaw -w jpg -ext CR3 .
    Creates jpg files from Canon raw (.CR3) files by extracting the embedded
    full-size JPEG preview. -b binary output, -JpgFromRaw the tag to extract,
    -w jpg write each to <name>.jpg, -ext CR3 only process CR3 files,
    . = current directory.
    $ exiftool -b -JpgFromRaw -w jpg -ext CR3 .
    $ exiftool IMG_0001.CR3                # show all metadata (camera, lens, ISO...)
    $ exiftool -all= photo.jpg             # strip all metadata (privacy)

ffmpeg
    Converts audio/video.
    $ ffmpeg -i input.mov -c:v libx264 output.mp4
    $ ffmpeg -i video.mp4 -vn audio.mp3    # extract audio


================================================================================
 19. GIT
================================================================================

git remote set-url origin git@github.com:jetam/FileViewer.git
    Changes the remote from https to ssh -> stops asking you for username and
    password and uses your ssh key instead.
    $ git remote -v                        # check current remote URLs first
    $ git remote set-url origin git@github.com:jetam/FileViewer.git

Most used
    $ git status                           # what changed
    $ git add -p                           # stage changes chunk by chunk
    $ git commit -m "message"
    $ git commit --amend                   # fix the last commit (before pushing)
    $ git push / git pull --rebase
    $ git log --oneline --graph --all      # compact history tree
    $ git diff / git diff --staged         # unstaged / staged changes

Branches
    $ git switch -c feature/login          # create + switch to new branch
    $ git switch master
    $ git branch -d feature/login          # delete local branch
    $ git merge feature/login
    $ git rebase master                    # replay current branch on master

Undo
    $ git restore file.txt                 # discard local changes in a file
    $ git restore --staged file.txt        # unstage
    $ git reset --soft HEAD~1              # undo last commit, keep changes
    $ git revert <hash>                    # new commit that undoes <hash>
    $ git stash / git stash pop            # park changes temporarily

Inspect
    $ git blame file.txt                   # who changed each line
    $ git show <hash>                      # one commit's changes
    $ git log -p -- path/file              # history of one file


================================================================================
 20. DOCKER
================================================================================

    Images = templates (built from a Dockerfile). Containers = running instances of an image.

docker images
    Lists the images stored locally (name, tag, size).
    $ docker images
    $ docker images | grep myapp

docker pull
    Downloads an image from a registry (Docker Hub by default) without running it.
    $ docker pull postgres:16              # download image

docker build
    Builds an image from a Dockerfile. -t gives it a name:tag, the last argument
    is the build context (folder sent to the build).
    $ docker build -t myapp:latest .       # build from Dockerfile in current dir
    $ docker build -f docker/Dockerfile.dev -t myapp:dev .   # other Dockerfile

docker rmi
    Removes an image (fails if a container still uses it).
    $ docker rmi myapp:latest              # remove image

docker ps
    Lists containers: their ID, image, status, and published ports.
    $ docker ps                            # running containers
    $ docker ps -a                         # all, including stopped

docker run
    Creates and starts a new container from an image.
    -d detached (background), -p host:container port, -e env var,
    --name container name, -it interactive terminal, --rm delete on exit,
    -v host_path:container_path mount a folder/volume.
    $ docker run -d --name db -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret postgres:16
    $ docker run -it --rm ubuntu bash      # interactive throwaway container
    $ docker run -v $(pwd):/app myapp      # mount current dir into container

docker stop / docker start / docker restart
    Stops (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10s), starts again, or restarts an
    existing container. Data inside the container is kept.
    $ docker stop db / docker start db / docker restart db

docker rm
    Removes a stopped container (and its data unless it was in a volume).
    $ docker rm db                         # remove a stopped container
    $ docker rm -f db                      # stop + remove in one go

docker logs
    Shows what the container wrote to stdout/stderr.
    $ docker logs -f db                    # follow container logs
    $ docker logs --tail 100 db            # only the last 100 lines

docker exec
    Runs a command inside a RUNNING container.
    $ docker exec -it db bash              # shell inside a running container (or sh)
    $ docker exec -it db psql -U postgres  # run a program inside

docker inspect
    Full low-level details of a container/image as JSON.
    $ docker inspect db                    # full config / IP / mounts as JSON
    $ docker inspect -f '{{.NetworkSettings.IPAddress}}' db

docker stats
    Live resource usage per container (like top).
    $ docker stats                         # live CPU / memory per container

docker compose
    Runs multi-container apps defined in docker-compose.yml (services,
    ports, volumes, networks) with one command.
    $ docker compose up -d                 # start everything in docker-compose.yml
    $ docker compose down                  # stop + remove (add -v to delete volumes)
    $ docker compose logs -f web
    $ docker compose up -d --build         # rebuild images first
    $ docker compose ps                    # status of the compose services

docker system
    Shows / cleans up the disk space used by Docker.
    $ docker system df                     # how much space docker uses
    $ docker system prune -a               # remove unused images/containers/networks


================================================================================
 21. KUBERNETES (kubectl)
================================================================================

    Tip: alias k=kubectl   and enable completion:  source <(kubectl completion bash)

Context / cluster
kubectl config get-contexts
    Lists all clusters/contexts in your kubeconfig (~/.kube/config); * = current.
    $ kubectl config get-contexts

kubectl config use-context
    Switches to another cluster.
    $ kubectl config use-context minikube

kubectl config set-context --current --namespace
    Sets the default namespace so you don't need -n every time.
    $ kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=dev

kubectl cluster-info
    Shows the API server address - quick check that the cluster is reachable.
    $ kubectl cluster-info

Looking at resources
kubectl get
    Lists resources (pods, deployments, services, nodes, ns, configmaps...).
    $ kubectl get pods                     # pods in the current namespace
    $ kubectl get pods -A                  # all namespaces
    $ kubectl get pods -o wide             # + node and pod IP
    $ kubectl get pods -w                  # watch for changes
    $ kubectl get deploy,svc,ingress -n dev
    $ kubectl get pod mypod -o yaml        # full definition

kubectl describe
    Detailed info + EVENTS for a resource - first thing to check when a pod is
    Pending / CrashLoopBackOff / ImagePullBackOff.
    $ kubectl describe pod mypod-7d9f8c-xk2lp

kubectl get events
    Recent cluster events, sorted.
    $ kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

Debugging pods
kubectl logs
    Prints container logs.
    $ kubectl logs mypod
    $ kubectl logs -f deploy/myapp         # follow logs of a deployment's pod
    $ kubectl logs mypod -c sidecar        # specific container in the pod
    $ kubectl logs mypod --previous        # logs of the crashed previous run

kubectl exec
    Runs a command / opens a shell inside a container.
    $ kubectl exec -it mypod -- bash       # (or sh if no bash in image)
    $ kubectl exec mypod -- env

kubectl port-forward
    Forwards a local port to a pod/service - access it at localhost.
    $ kubectl port-forward svc/myapp 8080:80   # localhost:8080 -> service port 80

kubectl cp
    Copies files to/from a pod.
    $ kubectl cp mypod:/app/log.txt ./log.txt

kubectl top
    CPU/memory usage (needs metrics-server).
    $ kubectl top pods
    $ kubectl top nodes

Creating / changing
kubectl apply -f
    Creates or updates resources from a YAML file or folder (declarative).
    $ kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
    $ kubectl apply -f k8s/                # all files in a folder

kubectl delete
    Deletes resources.
    $ kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml
    $ kubectl delete pod mypod             # deployment will create a new one

kubectl create (quick, imperative)
    $ kubectl create namespace dev
    $ kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx
    $ kubectl create secret generic db-pass --from-literal=password=secret
    $ kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > web.yaml
          # generate a YAML template without creating anything

kubectl edit
    Opens the live resource in your editor; saving applies the change.
    $ kubectl edit deploy myapp

Deployments
kubectl scale
    $ kubectl scale deploy myapp --replicas=3

kubectl set image
    $ kubectl set image deploy/myapp myapp=myrepo/myapp:1.2.0

kubectl rollout
    Status / history / undo / restart of deployments.
    $ kubectl rollout status deploy/myapp
    $ kubectl rollout history deploy/myapp
    $ kubectl rollout undo deploy/myapp    # back to previous version
    $ kubectl rollout restart deploy/myapp # restart all pods (e.g. new config)

Misc
    $ kubectl explain deployment.spec      # built-in docs for YAML fields
    $ kubectl api-resources                # all resource types + short names
    $ kubectl get secret db-pass -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d

Local clusters
    $ minikube start / minikube dashboard
    $ kind create cluster


================================================================================
 22. POSTGRES (psql)
================================================================================

psql --version
    Shows the installed psql client version.
    $ psql --version

sudo -u postgres psql
    Logs in as the default `postgres` superuser (runs psql as the linux user
    postgres - uses peer authentication, no password needed).
    $ sudo -u postgres psql

psql -h -U -d
    Connect to a specific host / user / database.
    $ psql -h localhost -U matej -d mydb
    $ psql "postgresql://matej:secret@localhost:5432/mydb"
    $ psql -d mydb -c "SELECT count(*) FROM users;"   # run one query and exit
    $ psql -d mydb -f schema.sql           # run a SQL file

Meta-commands (inside psql)
    \l          - list databases
    \c mydb     - connect to database mydb
    \dt         - list tables
    \d users    - describe table users (columns, types, indexes)
    \du         - list users (roles)
    \dn         - list schemas
    \x          - toggle expanded display (nice for wide rows)
    \timing     - show how long each query takes
    \i file.sql - run a SQL file
    \?          - help for meta-commands, \h SELECT - help for SQL command
    \q          - quit

Users & databases (SQL inside psql)
    CREATE USER matej WITH PASSWORD 'secret';
    CREATE DATABASE mydb OWNER matej;
    GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE mydb TO matej;
    ALTER USER matej WITH SUPERUSER;

Backup & restore
    $ pg_dump -U matej mydb > mydb.sql                 # dump to SQL
    $ psql -U matej -d mydb < mydb.sql                 # restore
    $ pg_dump -Fc mydb > mydb.dump ; pg_restore -d mydb mydb.dump   # custom format

Service
    $ sudo systemctl status postgresql

